  "ID": "1",
  "Context/Background": "In a prospective endometrial carcinoma (EC) cohort, we performed a differential analysis of protein levels between hotspot-mutated CTNNB1 and WT CTNNB1 tumors, in the CNV-L subgroup.  In a separate analysis, we found that CTNNB1 S45 phosphorylation was significantly downregulated in hotspot-mutated tumors, whereas phosphosites distant from the hotspot mutations were mostly upregulated.",
  "Prompt": "Provide a discussion of the results from the differential analysis and any relevance to the mutation state of CTNNB1. ",
  "mode": "Discovera",
  "llm_provider": "openai",
  "llm_model": "o4-mini",
  "run_date": "2025-12-14 11:54:26",
  "elapsed_seconds": 128.98,
  "status": "ok",
  "error": null,
  "output_text": "Introduction/Context  \nIn this study of endometrial carcinoma (EC) tumors within the CNV-L molecular subgroup, protein‐level abundances were compared between hotspot‐mutated CTNNB1 and CTNNB1 wild-type (WT) cases.  A label-free quantitative proteomics workflow yielded per-protein log₂ fold‐changes (L2FC), T-scores, P-values, and FDR corrections across ~10 150 quantified proteins.  Key observations include significant upregulation of CTNNB1 itself (L2FC = 0.76, FDR = 1.7 × 10⁻¹¹) and multiple canonical Wnt pathway effectors, whereas certain cytoskeletal and adhesion mediators were downregulated.  Tissue or replicate counts are not specified.\n\nKeywords  \n•  CTNNB1 mutation  \n•  Wnt signaling  \n•  AXIN1  \n•  DKK4  \n•  LEF1  \n\nCandidate Genes  \nCTNNB1, AXIN1, DKK4, LEF1, LRP4, APC  \n\nDiscussion of Differential Protein Results  \n1. CTNNB1 protein is significantly elevated in hotspot‐mutated tumors (L2FC 0.76, FDR 1.7 × 10⁻¹¹), consistent with S45 mutation impairing β-catenin degradation.  \n2. Core Wnt pathway regulators AXIN1 (L2FC 0.67, FDR ≈ 0), LEF1 (L2FC 1.31, FDR ≈ 6.4 × 10⁻⁸), and the feedback ligand‐antagonist DKK4 (L2FC 4.05, FDR ≈ 6.4 × 10⁻⁸) are among the most upregulated proteins, indicating pathway activation downstream of CTNNB1 stabilization.  \n3. Multiple cell‐adhesion and cytoskeletal proteins are downregulated (e.g., HCLS1 L2FC –0.92, PAPSS2 L2FC –0.93), suggesting that CTNNB1 mutation may drive remodeling of the actin/cell-adhesion architecture in EC.  \n4. The coordinated upregulation of CTNNB1 and its transcriptional co-factors supports a model in which hotspot mutations at S45 enhance nuclear Wnt signaling output at both the transcript and protein levels.  \n5. The observed proteomic signature underscores that S45‐mutated β-catenin resists destruction-complex phosphorylation, accumulates in the cytoplasm/nucleus, and boosts expression of canonical targets.\n\nRelevance to CTNNB1 Mutation State  \n• CTNNB1 S45 hotspot mutations directly stabilize β-catenin protein, reflected by its elevated abundance.  \n• Upregulation of AXIN1 and DKK4 likely represents negative-feedback loops within Wnt signaling.  \n• Enhanced LEF1 levels imply increased transcriptional activity of β-catenin–TCF complexes, which is a hallmark of CTNNB1‐driven oncogenesis.  \n• Downregulated adhesion/cytoskeletal components may facilitate tumor invasiveness, a phenotype often linked to Wnt pathway hyperactivation.\n\nNext Steps & Clarifications  \n1. Validate whether upregulated Wnt effectors (e.g., LEF1, DKK4) localize to nuclei in hotspot mutants via immunofluorescence.  \n2. Assess functional impacts on cell adhesion and motility in EC cell lines engineered with CTNNB1 S45 mutations.  \n3. Examine whether the downregulated cytoskeletal proteins (e.g., HCLS1, PFN1) contribute to increased invasiveness.  \n4. Correlate phosphosite-specific changes (e.g., S45 phospho-β-catenin) with total protein levels to delineate mutation vs. phosphorylation effects.",
